1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:04,750
Okay. Hey.

2
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:06,960
- Does everybody hate these shoes?     - Oh, yeah.

3
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:12,010
But I don't think anybody's gonna focus on that as long as you wear that towel dress.

4
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:13,970
Tell him.

5
00:00:14,130 --> 00:00:18,350
It's her first day at this new job. You're not supposed to start with her.

6
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:21,850
All right. I suppose I can wait a day.

7
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:24,810
- Hey, what are you doing Friday?     - Why?

8
00:00:24,980 --> 00:00:28,150
I need you to come to this bachelor party for my weird cousin Albert.

9
00:00:28,310 --> 00:00:29,820
You know, he's the botanist?

10
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:33,440
Oh, God. Yeah, botanists are such geeks.

11
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:35,280
Yeah.

12
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:36,490
Is that a dinosaur tie?

13
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:37,740
Hmm? Oh, yeah.

14
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:43,700
- Morning.     - Hey.

15
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:46,960
Hey. Rach, I'm here with the purses.

16
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,960
It must take you forever to find your keys.

17
00:00:51,130 --> 00:00:52,460
Thank you, thank you, Pheebs.

18
00:00:52,630 --> 00:00:55,470
Oh, you're welcome. Oh, please want the one with the turtles.

19
00:00:55,630 --> 00:00:58,430
No, no, no, turtles scare me. I don't need that today.

20
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:02,430
Honey, just relax. It's gonna be fine.

21
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:05,230
Hey, why don't I come down there and take you to lunch?

22
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:08,020
Aw, honey, thank you, but Mark's taking me out.

23
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:12,070
Mark? Is that the same Mark that helped you get the job?

24
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:15,690
Yeah. It's kind of like a "good luck on your first day" sort of thing.

25
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:17,200
Is this actually a lunch box?

26
00:01:17,410 --> 00:01:20,660
Um, no, it's a purse. And there's a thermos in it.

27
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:25,750
- Hey, so can you make it on Friday?     - What?

28
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:28,540
Oh, yeah, I think so. Why am I invited to this again?

29
00:01:28,710 --> 00:01:30,460
Apparently, Albert has no friends.

30
00:01:30,630 --> 00:01:32,420
He's excited about the bachelor party, though.

31
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:35,420
I think the only reason he's getting married is so he can see a stripper.

32
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:41,300
A stripper at a bachelor party. That is so cliche.

33
00:01:41,470 --> 00:01:44,180
Why don't you guys get a magician?

34
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:48,730
If a magician can open my beer with his butt cheeks, then all right.

35
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,500
# So no one told you life was gonna be this way #

36
00:02:00,070 --> 00:02:04,240
# Your job's a joke, you're broke, your love life's D.O.A. #

37
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:08,960
# It's like you're always stuck in second gear #

38
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:11,520
# When it hasn't been your day, #

39
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:14,520
# your week, your month, or even your year #

40
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:18,480
# but I'll be there for you #

41
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:20,310
# When the rain starts to pour #

42
00:02:20,310 --> 00:02:23,430
# I'll be there for you #

43
00:02:23,430 --> 00:02:25,270
# Like I've been there before #

44
00:02:25,270 --> 00:02:28,280
# I'll be there for you #

45
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:31,940
# 'Cause you're there for me too #

46
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:40,200
She's having lunch with him? Lunch with him?

47
00:02:40,410 --> 00:02:43,200
You should've seen the hug he gave her when she got the job.

48
00:02:43,370 --> 00:02:46,080
And he's really good-looking.

49
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:50,000
- What am I gonna do?     - You don't do anything.

50
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:52,790
Keep it inside. Learn how to hide your feelings.

51
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:55,710
Don't cry out loud.

52
00:02:57,840 --> 00:02:59,510
Yes! Unh!

53
00:02:59,670 --> 00:03:02,010
Guess who has an audition for a Broadway musical.

54
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:05,720
I wanna say you, but it seems like such an easy answer.

55
00:03:06,180 --> 00:03:07,260
It is me.

56
00:03:07,430 --> 00:03:10,230
It's a musical version of A Tale of Two Cities.

57
00:03:10,390 --> 00:03:12,690
So I think I'm gonna sing "New York, New York"...

58
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:16,770
...and, uh, oh, "I Left My Heart in San Francisco."

59
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:23,200
Uh, Joey, I don't think you get to pick the cities.

60
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:27,080
- What?     - Mr. Dickens gets to pick them.

61
00:03:28,790 --> 00:03:30,250
Who?

62
00:03:31,370 --> 00:03:33,330
I'll get you the CliffsNotes.

63
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:35,290
The what?

64
00:03:35,790 --> 00:03:36,920
The abridgment.

65
00:03:37,090 --> 00:03:40,130
Oh, okay. Ha, ha.

66
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:42,300
The what?

67
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:48,560
Then the style number...

68
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:52,770
...the invoice number, and the shipping date.

69
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:54,900
Good. Any questions so far?

70
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:57,310
Yeah. What kind of discount do we get?

71
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:02,030
- Ha, ha. Twenty percent.     - Oh. I love this job. Ha, ha.

72
00:04:03,610 --> 00:04:05,160
- Ah! My first call.     - Here, let me.

73
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:07,030
Okay.

74
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:10,200
- Rachel Green's line. How may I help you?     - Hi, is Rachel there?

75
00:04:10,370 --> 00:04:13,330
- And who may I say is calling?     - This is Ross.

76
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:14,540
Ross of?

77
00:04:15,330 --> 00:04:18,090
Of "Ross and Rachel."

78
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:20,380
Oh. Hi, it's Mark.

79
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:22,590
- Oh, hey, Mark. Heh. Hey.     - Hey.

80
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:24,090
- Hey, hold on a second.     - Uh, okay.

81
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:25,430
- It's Ross.     - Okay.

82
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:27,890
- Hi, honey.     - Hi.

83
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:30,680
What's Mark doing answering your phone?

84
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:32,680
Oh, he's just goofing around.

85
00:04:32,850 --> 00:04:35,770
Oh, that's funny.

86
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:40,070
Why isn't he goofing around in his own office?

87
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:44,320
Ha, ha. Honey, this is his office too. I told you, we're Joanna's two assistants.

88
00:04:44,950 --> 00:04:46,740
Why does Joanna need two assistants?

89
00:04:46,910 --> 00:04:49,200
How lazy is she?

90
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:52,410
- Oh. Oh, my God. What did I just do?     - What?

91
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:54,710
I think I just shipped 3000 bras to Personnel.

92
00:04:54,870 --> 00:04:56,710
Honey, I gotta go. Mark, I need you.

93
00:04:56,870 --> 00:04:59,130
Okay. Bye-bye.

94
00:04:59,790 --> 00:05:01,750
Ow! Ross!

95
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:04,420
Huh? Oh. Oh, I'm sorry, sweetie. I was just trying to...

96
00:05:04,590 --> 00:05:07,470
Uh, I was dialing another number.

97
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:24,280
All right, I just got changed in 30 seconds so you could be alone with him.

98
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:28,070
- You better go for it.     - Please. I'm not going for anything.

99
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:31,620
- Well, if you don't, I will.     - Would you please go?

100
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:34,040
Night, Mon.

101
00:05:35,370 --> 00:05:37,500
Night, Julio.

102
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:50,800
Look, Julio, someone left their book here.

103
00:05:50,970 --> 00:05:52,720
Actually, that is mine.

104
00:05:52,890 --> 00:05:54,220
- Oh, yeah?     - Yes.

105
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:55,810
What are you reading?

106
00:05:55,980 --> 00:05:59,520
Flowers of Evil by Baudelaire. Have you read it?

107
00:05:59,690 --> 00:06:02,440
Ha, ha. Have I read it?

108
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:05,860
No. Are you enjoying it?

109
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:09,950
I thought that I would, but the translation's no good.

110
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:13,080
You're a poet and you don't know it.

111
00:06:16,950 --> 00:06:20,210
Actually, I, uh... I am a poet.

112
00:06:22,790 --> 00:06:24,880
So you do know it. Ha, ha.

113
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:33,350
Ahem. So, uh, what kind of things do you write about?

114
00:06:33,510 --> 00:06:35,720
Things that move me.

115
00:06:35,890 --> 00:06:37,930
The shadow of a tree...

116
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:40,230
...a child laughing...

117
00:06:42,190 --> 00:06:43,360
...or this lip.

118
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:45,520
Mine?

119
00:06:46,030 --> 00:06:47,650
Right here?

120
00:06:48,950 --> 00:06:53,450
I could write an epic poem about this lip.

121
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:01,120
How would that go?

122
00:07:11,430 --> 00:07:14,140
Well, it didn't rhyme, ha, ha...

123
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:15,390
...but I liked it.

124
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:24,020
# You've got to pick a pocket or two, boys #

125
00:07:24,230 --> 00:07:31,190
# You've got to pick a pocket or two #

126
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:37,450
Lovely. Just lovely.

127
00:07:37,620 --> 00:07:39,240
Really? Thanks.

128
00:07:39,410 --> 00:07:42,910
Listen, Joey, we definitely wanna see you for the callback on Saturday.

129
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:44,370
Excellent.

130
00:07:44,580 --> 00:07:46,330
- I'll be there.     - Ha, ha. Okay.

131
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:49,920
And listen, don't forget to bring your jazz shoes for the dance audition.

132
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:57,430
Uh, my agent said that it wasn't a dancing part.

133
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:00,470
Joey, all the roles gotta dance a little. Ha, ha.

134
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:03,810
But believe me, with your dance background, it'll be a piece of cake.

135
00:08:03,980 --> 00:08:07,270
Three years of modern dance with Twyla Tharp?

136
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:11,230
Five years with the American Ballet Theatre?

137
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:16,700
Hey, everybody lies on their resume, okay?

138
00:08:16,870 --> 00:08:19,580
I wasn't one of the Zoom kids either.

139
00:08:20,370 --> 00:08:22,620
Well, can you, like, dance at all?

140
00:08:22,830 --> 00:08:25,250
Yeah, I can dance. You know:

141
00:08:33,670 --> 00:08:36,300
Oh, no, no, no.

142
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:39,010
What is that?

143
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:42,350
Sure, it looks stupid now. There's no music playing.

144
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:46,480
All right, I have to get that. But no, no.

145
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:49,310
Hello?

146
00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:56,910
Yeah, listen, I'm in need of a stripper, and I was told that you do that.

147
00:09:00,540 --> 00:09:03,870
Let me ask you this. What do you do for the extra hundred?

148
00:09:05,330 --> 00:09:09,000
So would I have to provide the grapes?

149
00:09:09,630 --> 00:09:11,170
Hi.

150
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:13,260
How was last night with Julio, señorita? Ha, ha.

151
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:14,880
It was so amazing.

152
00:09:15,050 --> 00:09:18,130
He's so sexy and smart, which makes him even sexier.

153
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:20,890
Oh, my gosh. Last night we were fooling around...

154
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:24,100
...and all of a sudden he stops to write a poem.

155
00:09:24,270 --> 00:09:27,850
Get out. I couldn't stop if a meteor hit me.

156
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:34,900
Okay, we have our stripper, a Miss Crystal Chandelier.

157
00:09:35,070 --> 00:09:38,530
Well, sure. You name a kid that, what do you expect them to grow up to be?

158
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:43,660
Ahem. Anyway, when he left he forgot to take the poem with him.

159
00:09:43,830 --> 00:09:48,420
Now, I am totally dense about poetry, but I think this is pretty good, all right?

160
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:51,330
Check it out. Read it.

161
00:09:52,090 --> 00:09:53,840
"The Empty Vase."

162
00:09:54,010 --> 00:09:55,170
Translucent beauty...

163
00:09:55,340 --> 00:09:57,170
To yourself.

164
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:03,890
- Hey, you know, that's pretty good.     - Yeah, I think so too. Phoebe?

165
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:06,560
- Oh, yeah.     - Oh, great. I'm so glad you guys like it.

166
00:10:06,730 --> 00:10:09,770
- Yay. All right, I gotta go to work.     - No, no. I'm not done.

167
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:12,650
Give it back when you're through.

168
00:10:12,820 --> 00:10:14,440
- Okay, see you guys.     - Bye-bye.

169
00:10:14,610 --> 00:10:15,940
Bye.

170
00:10:16,110 --> 00:10:18,450
Oh, my God. Oh, my God. Poor Monica.

171
00:10:18,610 --> 00:10:20,910
- What? What? What?     - What?

172
00:10:21,070 --> 00:10:23,490
He was with her when he wrote this poem. Look.

173
00:10:23,660 --> 00:10:26,490
My vessel so lovely With nothing inside

174
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:29,580
Now that I've touched you You seem emptier still

175
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:32,670
He thinks Monica's empty. She's the empty vase.

176
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:36,210
- You really think that's what he meant?     - Totally.

177
00:10:36,380 --> 00:10:39,130
Oh, God. Oh, and she seems so happy too.

178
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:41,180
Done.

179
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:48,800

180
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:54,020
Do you have the Ralph Lauren file?

181
00:10:54,190 --> 00:10:56,940
Oh, yeah, sure. It's, uh right...

182
00:11:01,820 --> 00:11:03,280
What's that?

183
00:11:03,450 --> 00:11:05,740
Uh, it's from Ross. It's a love bug.

184
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:10,460
Wow. Somebody wants people to know you have a boyfriend.

185
00:11:10,620 --> 00:11:13,630
Oh, no. No, no, no. That's not what he's doing.

186
00:11:13,790 --> 00:11:16,670
- He's just really romantic.     - Okay.

187
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:19,130
Excuse me. Are you Rachel Green?

188
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:20,510
Yes, I am.

189
00:11:21,880 --> 00:11:22,970
One, two, three.

190
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:26,810
# Congratulations on your first week at your brand-new job. #

191
00:11:26,970 --> 00:11:28,770
# It won't be long Before you're the boss #

192
00:11:30,180 --> 00:11:32,890
# And you know who will be there To support you #

193
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:34,480
# Your one and only boyfriend #

194
00:11:34,650 --> 00:11:36,270
# It's nice to have a boyfriend. #

195
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:40,320
# Your loyal, loving boyfriend, Ross Ross! #

196
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:48,790
I'm hurt. I'm actually hurt...

197
00:11:48,950 --> 00:11:53,000
...that you'd think I'd send you those things out of anything other than love.

198
00:11:53,170 --> 00:11:54,790
Hurt.

199
00:11:55,540 --> 00:11:58,250
- Hurt.     - All right, Ross, I get it. You're hurt.

200
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:03,760
Can't a guy send a barbershop quartet to his girlfriend's office anymore?

201
00:12:05,260 --> 00:12:07,930
Oh, please. Ross, it was so obvious.

202
00:12:08,100 --> 00:12:10,100
It was like you were marking your territory.

203
00:12:10,270 --> 00:12:14,850
I mean, you might as well have just come in and peed all around my desk.

204
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:18,440
I would never do that.

205
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:23,030
Look, I know what's going on here, okay?

206
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:27,490
Mark explained it all to me. He said this is what you guys do.

207
00:12:27,660 --> 00:12:31,490
Yeah, well, if Mark said that, then Mark's an idiot.

208
00:12:31,660 --> 00:12:33,000
Mark's a genius.

209
00:12:33,160 --> 00:12:37,130
- Why? How? How is he a genius?     - Look, don't you see what's happening?

210
00:12:37,290 --> 00:12:40,420
Instead of hitting on her right away, he's becoming her confidant.

211
00:12:40,590 --> 00:12:44,050
Now he's gonna be the guy that she goes to to complain about you.

212
00:12:44,220 --> 00:12:45,430
What am I gonna do?

213
00:12:45,590 --> 00:12:47,550
Well, why don't you send her a musical bug?

214
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:49,970
Oh, no, you already did that.

215
00:12:51,930 --> 00:12:54,980
All right, look, you're gonna have to go there yourself now, okay?

216
00:12:55,140 --> 00:12:57,770
- Make a few surprise visits.     - I don't know, you guys.

217
00:12:57,940 --> 00:13:00,440
Fine, don't do anything. Just sit here and talk to us.

218
00:13:00,610 --> 00:13:04,940
Meanwhile, she is talking to him about you. And he's being Mr. Joe-Sensitive.

219
00:13:05,110 --> 00:13:09,280
She starts thinking, "Maybe this is the guy for me because he understands me."

220
00:13:09,450 --> 00:13:11,450
And before you know it, she's with him.

221
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:14,450
And you'll be all, "Oh, man."

222
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:16,790
And he'll be all, "Yes!"

223
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,630
And us, we'll be like, "Oh, dude."

224
00:13:23,630 --> 00:13:26,010
And pretty soon you'll be like:

225
00:13:26,180 --> 00:13:28,260
"Hi."

226
00:13:29,090 --> 00:13:34,220
And, "Well, I can't go. Rachel and Mark might be there."

227
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:38,140
And we'll be like, "Man, get over it. It's been four years."

228
00:13:40,610 --> 00:13:43,270
He paints quite a picture, doesn't he?

229
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:48,610
Here's the Shelli Segal stuff for December.

230
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:51,780
- Oh, wait, I've got something for you.     - What? Oh... Mark.

231
00:13:51,950 --> 00:13:53,830
It's okay. Rachel knows.

232
00:13:53,990 --> 00:13:56,160
Yeah, but even so... Ha, ha.

233
00:13:56,330 --> 00:13:59,540
I can't help it. I'm just crazy about you.

234
00:13:59,710 --> 00:14:02,790
Oh, that is so sweet.

235
00:14:03,750 --> 00:14:04,800
Okay, okay.

236
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:07,720
I know I'm Mr. Inappropriate today, but it's just so tough.

237
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:11,050
I see you walking around, and I just wanna touch and hold you.

238
00:14:11,260 --> 00:14:14,010
Come on, no one's around. Just kiss me.

239
00:14:14,180 --> 00:14:17,850
- All right, that's it. Get off. Get off of her.     - What is going on?

240
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:20,810
What's going on? I'll tell you what's going on.

241
00:14:21,020 --> 00:14:22,860
Ross.

242
00:14:27,400 --> 00:14:31,950
I have been down in your store for 20 minutes, trying to get a tie.

243
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:36,790
What do I have to do to get some service?

244
00:14:38,710 --> 00:14:40,420
Hi, Rach.

245
00:14:48,220 --> 00:14:50,840
Uh, Joey? Joey Tribbiani?

246
00:14:56,310 --> 00:15:00,020
